];

const faq = [
  { q: 'What sound does a raven make?', a: 'The Common Raven\'s signature call is a deep, resonant "kronk," "quork," or "crruk" — lower-pitched and more resonant than any crow or other corvid in its range. This deep "kronk" is the single most reliable sound identification feature separating ravens from crows in Europe and North America. Ravens also produce over 30 documented call types including alarm calls, food calls, contact calls, and can mimic other birds, mammals, and human speech.' },
  { q: 'How is a raven call different from a crow call?', a: 'The Common Raven produces a much deeper, more resonant call than the Carrion Crow or American Crow — often written "kronk" vs "caw" or "kaa." The raven\'s call carries further and has a hollow, resonant quality that sounds clearly different once heard. The pitch and resonance difference is the single most useful field identification feature when a bird is heard but not seen.' },
];
---

