    <p class="id-pillar">Questions · Identification</p>
    <h1>What Sound Does a Raven Make?</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Calls, vocalisations, and how to identify ravens by sound</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven's signature call is a <strong>deep, resonant "kronk" or "quork"</strong> — lower-pitched and more resonant than any crow. This "kronk" carries far and is the single best sound ID feature separating ravens from crows when the bird is out of sight. Ravens also produce 30+ other call types and are skilled vocal mimics.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Diagnostic "Kronk" Call</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The most commonly transcribed Common Raven call:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"Kronk"</strong> — deep, hollow, resonant; the classic field identification call</li>
      <li>Also transcribed as "quork," "crruk," "pruk," "cr-r-ruck"</li>
      <li>Typically 1–3 syllables; often given in series of 2–4</li>
      <li>Much lower-pitched than any crow call — a skilled observer can identify ravens by call alone in the field reliably</li>
      <li>Carries over long distances in open country</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This call is a primary identification feature separating Common Ravens from Carrion Crows, Hooded Crows, Rooks, and Jackdaws in Europe — and from American Crows in North America.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Other Common Calls</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens have an extensive vocal repertoire (30+ documented call types). Common categories include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Alarm call:</strong> More rapid, higher-pitched repeated calls; "quorrrk-quorrrk-quorrrk" in rapid succession</li>
      <li><strong>Food call / recruitment call:</strong> Distinctive call used to attract other ravens to a food source; described as "yelling" by Heinrich; different from the standard kronk</li>
      <li><strong>Territorial call:</strong> Deep, resonant calls given from prominent perches at territory boundaries; often in duet by pairs</li>
      <li><strong>Contact call:</strong> Soft "tok" or "klok" sounds used between paired birds at close range</li>
      <li><strong>Begging call:</strong> High, nasal calls given by young birds and females soliciting food from males</li>
      <li><strong>Play call:</strong> Variable clicking, gurgling, and bell-like sounds associated with play behaviour</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Vocal Mimicry</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are vocal learners — they can acquire and produce new sounds throughout their lives:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Mimic other bird species in their environment</li>
      <li>Mimic wolves, dogs, and other mammals</li>
      <li>Mimic human speech (captive birds; multiple documented cases including Edgar Allen Poe's pet raven Grip and Dickens's raven)</li>
      <li>Mimic environmental sounds (water, machinery)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Mimicry in wild ravens is documented but its frequency and function in natural contexts are not fully characterised. In captive ravens, mimicry of human words and other sounds is well-attested.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Raven vs Crow: Sound Comparison</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <table class="id-compare-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Species</th><th>Call transcription</th><th>Quality</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Common Raven</td><td>"Kronk," "quork," "crruk"</td><td>Very deep, resonant, hollow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Carrion Crow</td><td>"Kaa," "karrr"</td><td>Harsh, flat, mid-pitched</td></tr>
        <tr><td>American Crow</td><td>"Caw," "kaa"</td><td>Nasal, harsh, variable pitch</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rook</td><td>"Kaah," "karrr"</td><td>Raucous, deeper than crow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jackdaw</td><td>"Chak," "jack"</td><td>High, sharp, clearly higher than raven</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The raven's call is immediately distinguishable from all crows and from jackdaws once a listener is familiar with it. The resonance — sometimes compared to a wooden percussion instrument — is not achieved by any crow.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
